return await window.__createIslands([{"id":"ess-33","kind":"prose","x":5800,"y":2600,"w":460,"html":"<div style=\"font-family:'nimbus sans l',sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#000\"><p>A page receives ink. A photographic plate receives light. A wall receives projection. A magnetic drive receives difference. These familiar models encourage us to imagine inscription as something that happens on or in a substrate. But energetic memory suggests another possibility: inscription without surface, or at least without surface as the dominant figure. A trace may be distributed, residual, environmental, field-like, or detectable only through the consequences it leaves in a system. It may not resemble a mark. It may not be locatable as an object. It may persist as altered relation. This has consequences for art. Documentation can no longer be understood only as the preservation of what appeared. Conservation can no longer mean only the care of material components. Interpretation can no longer restrict itself to visible form or stated concept. Works that operate through imperceptible energies require an expanded account of trace, one that includes decay, propagation, disturbance, modulation, and the possibility of effects that exceed both spectatorship and archive. The question is not simply where the mark is. The question is what kind of world can keep it, and by what apparatuses such keeping becomes knowable.</p></div>"},{"id":"ess-34","kind":"prose","x":6400,"y":1400,"w":460,"html":"<div style=\"font-family:'nimbus sans l',sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#000\"><p>How does one conserve a work that is not primarily an object? One may preserve diagrams, technical specifications, code, devices, installation views, captions, models, recordings, correspondence, and written accounts. These are necessary, but they do not exhaust the work. In field-based or energy-based practices, the work may also reside in relations: between transmitter and receiver, rod and storm, ground and charge, seismometer and Earth, carrier and modulation, simulation and mathematical model, signal and possible reception. Conservation becomes less like preserving an artefact and more like maintaining, describing, or reactivating conditions. Yet not all conditions can be reactivated. A transmission that has already departed cannot be retrieved as the same event. A radioactive decay curve cannot be reset without changing the work's ontology. A missed reception cannot be restored as if it had occurred. Field-like conservation must therefore include irreversibility. It must attend not only to what can be repeated, but to what has entered the world and cannot be called back. This gives such works their strange temporal force. They are not simply documented after the fact. They continue as altered conditions, as residues of operations, as remembered energies.</p></div>"},{"id":"ess-35","kind":"prose","x":2000,"y":1080,"w":460,"html":"<div style=\"font-family:'nimbus sans l',sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#000\"><p>The antenna should not be reduced to metaphor, although it inevitably attracts metaphorical thought. It is too exact for metaphor alone. An antenna receives, transmits, resonates, filters, converts, and distributes. Its form is shaped by wavelength, frequency, conductivity, impedance, directionality, gain, environment, and purpose. It belongs simultaneously to biology, engineering, communication, warfare, astronomy, domestic technology, ecology, and art. Biological antennae extend the body into chemical, tactile, and vibrational environments; engineered antennae extend technical systems into electromagnetic space. In both cases, sensing is not merely a mental or optical act but a morphological arrangement. To become sensitive, a body or device must take shape in relation to what it seeks to detect. This is why antennae matter philosophically. They materialise relation. They demonstrate that reception is never passive and that transmission is never pure departure. An antenna does not simply open itself to the world. It selects, shapes, privileges, excludes, and transforms. It is a form of attention made material. To think with antennae is therefore to think of art not as an object offered to perception, but as an apparatus configured for relation with forces that exceed unaided sense.</p></div>"}]);